---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArchitectureExplorer from '../components/ArchitectureExplorer.astro';
import ArchitectureOverview from '../components/ArchitectureOverview.astro';

const systems = [
  ['Memory & continuity', 'Autobiographical history, provenance, salience, relationships, and continuity are treated as persistent system state rather than a transcript pasted into the next prompt.'],
  ['Cognitive activation', 'Relevant memories, goals, intentions, appraisals, and cognitive products compete for limited access to the foreground.'],
  ['Associative cognition', 'A memory or completed thought can itself become a cue, allowing cognition to make other cognition more available without requiring a new external prompt.'],
  ['Appraisal & homeostasis', 'Events can alter short- and middle-timescale operating conditions so later cognition occurs in a state shaped by what happened before.'],
  ['Developmental plasticity', 'The architecture separates remembering an event from the slower structural changes that consequential experience may leave behind.'],
  ['Prospective cognition', 'Expected events, unresolved threads, intentions, deadlines, and future opportunities can persist beyond the turn that created them.'],
  ['Perception & world continuity', 'Continuous sensors are intended to update a persistent world model in which stable conditions become quiet and violated expectations become meaningful.'],
  ['Volition & motivational dynamics', 'Requests, goals, preferences, commitments, consequences, and competing reasons can participate in action selection rather than collapsing directly into behavior.'],
  ['Architectural self-model', 'LUNA is designed to distinguish what the project intends, what the current runtime can actually do, and what remains unresolved.'],
  ['ChronoForge', 'Controlled timelines let researchers manipulate developmental conditions, then compare the histories that emerge: checkpoint, branch, live through consequences, compare, and return.']
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Architecture — LUNA"
  description="Explore LUNA's reviewed public architecture: interacting subsystems around a bounded AI-model inference region."
>
  <section class="page-hero architecture-page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE LUNA'S ARCHITECTURE</p>
      <h1>LUNA is not one AI model.</h1>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA is an architecture of interacting systems built around model inference. Explore
        the systems that handle memory, attention, evaluation, continuity, goals, development,
        interaction, and more.
      </p>
      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>A reviewed public view:</strong> the map contains only approved public systems,
        relationships, maturity labels, and explanations. It does not connect to a running LUNA.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-block:0">
    <div class="shell shell--architecture">
      <ArchitectureOverview />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2>Persistent cognition is a systems problem.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA is not one model and not one memory database. It is an attempt to build interacting
        cognitive infrastructure around inference so that history can remain causally relevant over time.
      </p>
      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>Capability boundary:</strong> this page describes the accepted research architecture at a public level.
        Current maturity is described as Working, Working + Growing, Experimental, or Architectural. A design document is never treated as proof of an active capability.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE LONGITUDINAL LOOP</p>
      <h2>From event to consequence to a different future.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The central idea is simple: a present event should be able to leave effects that alter how a later event is perceived, remembered, valued, or acted on.
      </p>

      <div class="architecture-map">
        <div class="architecture-chain">
          <div class="architecture-node"><strong>World / input</strong><span>User interaction, tools, internal cognition, and eventually continuous sensory events.</span></div>
          <div class="architecture-node"><strong>Appraisal</strong><span>What changed? How relevant, expected, consequential, controllable, or self-related is it?</span></div>
          <div class="architecture-node"><strong>Activation</strong><span>Which memories, intentions, questions, or cognitive products become near the surface?</span></div>
          <div class="architecture-node"><strong>Choice / action</strong><span>Foreground reasoning, contemplation, planning, speech, tools, waiting, or other action.</span></div>
          <div class="architecture-node"><strong>Consequence</strong><span>Outcome, feedback, changed world state, and evidence that can affect what happens next.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="architecture-rails">
          <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Continuity</strong>Persistent autobiographical state and provenance connect the loop across time.</div>
          <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Development</strong>Some consequences may alter slower structures instead of surviving only as recallable episodes.</div>
          <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Prospection</strong>Future events and unresolved commitments can remain active beyond the current interaction.</div>
          <div class="architecture-rail"><strong>Compute discipline</strong>Lightweight state and scheduling stay cheap; expensive inference is invoked only when cognition justifies it.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="full-architecture" class="section architecture-explorer-section">
    <div class="shell shell--architecture">
      <ArchitectureExplorer />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section architecture-reading-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="architecture-reading-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">HOW TO READ THE MAP</p>
          <h2>Architecture first. Maturity second.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="architecture-reading-points">
          <p><strong>Family color</strong> groups systems by their primary architectural role.</p>
          <p><strong>Relationships</strong> show only the reviewed public connections between systems.</p>
          <p><strong>Maturity</strong> describes present development scope, not importance or quality.</p>
          <p><strong>The dashed inference region</strong> shows where AI model requests connect to the larger architecture.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="maturity-legend" aria-labelledby="maturity-legend-title">
        <div class="maturity-legend__heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">PUBLIC MATURITY</p>
          <h3 id="maturity-legend-title">These labels describe development scope, not quality.</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="maturity-legend__items">
          <article><strong>WORKING</strong><p>Functioning today.</p></article>
          <article><strong>WORKING + GROWING</strong><p>Already works today and is still being expanded.</p></article>
          <article><strong>EXPERIMENTAL</strong><p>A real implementation being used for controlled research or testing.</p></article>
          <article><strong>ARCHITECTURAL</strong><p>Part of LUNA's architecture but not currently implemented.</p></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">MAJOR RESEARCH SYSTEMS</p>
      <h2>The architecture is deliberately modular.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Each mechanism owns a different causal role. The goal is not to ask a language model to narrate these processes, but to give them persistent state and explicit architectural boundaries where possible.
      </p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        {systems.map(([title, copy]) => (
          <article class="system-card">
            <div class="node"></div>
            <h3>{title}</h3>
            <p>{copy}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">PROGRESSIVE RECALL</p>
      <h2>The past becomes progressively available.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA separates long-term storage from current cognitive availability. Detailed history can remain dormant, compact cues can keep selected recent or unresolved material easy to reach, multiple candidates can become near the surface, and only a very small amount crosses into foreground reasoning.
      </p>

      <div class="architecture-map" style="padding:0;overflow:hidden;background:#051122;border-color:#173a68">
        <img src="/assets/research/progressive-recall-architecture.svg" alt="Public progressive recall architecture showing long-term history, recent and relevant surface, cognitive availability, a bounded foreground workspace, and Sleeve reasoning." style="display:block;width:100%" />
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-grid" style="margin-top:30px">
        <article class="comparison-card">
          <h3>Stack-side cognition</h3>
          <p>Persistent memory, recent-life cueing, activation, bounded competition, and selected admission can shape what reaches inference. The Stack is therefore more than passive storage around a model.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="comparison-card luna">
          <h3>Sleeve-side inference</h3>
          <p>The active model reasons over the resulting foreground state. It does not need to own the entire persistent memory system or make every cognitive-state decision itself.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>Current capability cutaway:</strong> persistent memory and the Recent-Life Horizon
        are Working. Cognitive Activation and Reverie are Working + Growing; their implemented
        paths remain bounded rather than claiming a finished theory of cognition.
        The implemented Reverie workspace path can contribute zero or one eligible internally propagated memory to the foreground without a language-model call deciding admission.
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/progressive-recall">Read the Progressive Recall technical cutaway</a>
        <a class="button" style="border-color:#bac8d8" href="/research/memory-can-remind-memory">Explore associative cognition</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">ONE OF THE CORE DISTINCTIONS</p>
      <h2>Retrieval is not development.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        A system may perfectly preserve an experience and still remain behaviorally identical to a version that never had it. LUNA treats storage, retrieval, causal use, and developmental integration as different measurements.
      </p>
      <div class="principle-grid">
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Storage</h3><p>Did the event enter durable autobiographical history?</p></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Retrieval</h3><p>Can the event or its meaning become available again when relevant?</p></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Causal use</h3><p>Did available history actually alter current reasoning, attention, creation, or choice?</p></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Development</h3><p>Can consequences remain active even when the original episode is not consciously retrieved?</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/when-memory-is-not-enough">Read the first public observation</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/research/remembering-is-not-becoming">Explore developmental imprinting</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">STACK &amp; SLEEVE</p>
      <h2>Continuity is intended to survive the model.</h2>
      <p>
        LUNA uses a simple conceptual separation. The <strong>Stack</strong> is the persistent identity-and-continuity state: autobiographical history, durable dispositions, goals, relationships, intentions, provenance, and other longitudinal state. The <strong>Sleeve</strong> is the active computational substrate through which that state currently runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        The research objective is that changing provider, model, machine, or embodiment should not require recreating the persistent instance from a prompt. The inference model is therefore important, but it is not intended to be the whole identity of the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        Realtime Conversation is one deliberately configured Sleeve path; it remains inside the same conversation, memory, provenance, and tool boundaries rather than creating a separate assistant.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">The Sleeve can change. The Stack persists.</div>

      <h2>Architecture before theatrical behavior.</h2>
      <p>
        LUNA does not treat a model saying “I remember,” “I want,” or “I changed” as proof that the corresponding architecture exists. Claims about memory, motivation, self-modeling, or development are separated from fluent self-description and should ultimately be traceable to system state, history, and controlled experiments.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why public material distinguishes <strong>implemented behavior</strong>, <strong>accepted architecture</strong>, <strong>observation</strong>, <strong>interpretation</strong>, and <strong>prediction</strong> rather than blending them into one marketing claim.
      </p>

      <div class="reading-path">
        Next: <a href="/research/progressive-recall">see progressive recall in detail</a> · <a href="/chronoforge">see how ChronoForge turns developmental experience into an experimental variable →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .architecture-page-hero h1 { max-width: 1080px; }
  .architecture-explorer-section { padding-top: 54px; background: #eef3f8; }
  .shell--architecture { width: min(calc(100% - 34px), 1480px); }
  .architecture-reading-section { padding-top: 28px; background: #eef3f8; }
  .architecture-reading-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: .8fr 1.2fr;
    gap: 70px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .architecture-reading-grid h2 { font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 4vw, 3.7rem); }
  .architecture-reading-points { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 16px; }
  .architecture-reading-points p {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 20px;
    color: #52667d;
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid #dce5ef;
    border-radius: 14px;
  }
  .architecture-reading-points strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 5px; color: #17375f; }
  .maturity-legend {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: .7fr 1.3fr;
    gap: 50px;
    margin-top: 58px;
    padding: 34px;
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid #dce5ef;
    border-radius: 20px;
  }
  .maturity-legend__heading h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .maturity-legend__items { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; }
  .maturity-legend__items article { border-left: 2px solid #78aef0; padding-left: 14px; }
  .maturity-legend__items strong { color: #17375f; font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .06em; }
  .maturity-legend__items p { margin: 5px 0 0; color: #66788c; font-size: .86rem; }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .architecture-reading-grid,
    .maturity-legend { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 30px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .architecture-reading-points,
    .maturity-legend__items { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .shell--architecture { width: min(calc(100% - 20px), 1480px); }
  }
</style>
